<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gascony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gascony is a historic region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the Atlantic. Its name derives from the Vascones, Basque-speaking people who extended their rule north of the Pyrenees in the late 6th century. A duchy from the 7th century, it passed with Aquitaine to the English crown in 1152 and was held by English kings until the French conquest of 1453.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gascony.jpg" alt="Gascony" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Larrousiney · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MapOfGascony.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
